<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Suan Dok</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1371</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Suan Dok is a Buddhist temple in Chiang Mai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, built around 1371 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kue-na">Kue Na</a> in his flower garden for the monk Sumana, who founded there a monastic lineage derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. Its great bell-shaped chedi is said to hold a relic of the Buddha, and white memorial chedis contain the ashes of Chiang Mai's ruling family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-suan-dok.jpg" alt="Wat Suan Dok" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Setawut · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Suan_Dok_2026_(325013).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
